        "id": 1314845
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-09T21:20:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think the only reason for `inline` in a `.c` file (as opposed to a header file) would be to tell the compiler to inline the function. That\u0027s not really necessary here (over-optimization) and [it may not work anyway](https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/language/inline):\n\n\u003e The intent of the inline specifier is to serve as a hint for the compiler to perform optimizations, such as function inlining, which usually require the definition of a function to be visible at the call site. The compilers can (and usually do) ignore presence or absence of the inline specifier for the purpose of optimization.\n\nSome additional details on `inline` in C++ [here](http://go/totw/125#summary), though that\u0027s just for reference since this is C.",
      "parentUuid": "2ee90ea8_04d29699",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 34,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 34,
        "endChar": 33
      },
